poly-editor overlay on the tracker-ff firmware sources (scripts/vendor.sh applies it with patch -p1 to vendor/poly, after the byte for byte copy).
It keeps only what the editor really does beyond the firmware, and nothing the firmware already has (ids, stored numbers, engine tables, the
.patch codec are all the firmware's own at the tag: test/sync.test.ts checks that). Three things, each marked "poly-editor overlay" in the code:
  1. open_engine.h, engine_atype.cpp, open_synth.cpp: a held-note registry (SynthParams::held) and Poly 1 note priority (A-Type Priority
     Last / Low / High, A-Type Keyboard Duo). The firmware never fills SynthParams::held, so on the Tracker these two do nothing.
  2. open_engine.h, open_synth.cpp: kEditorMaxVoices 8 voice records per instrument (the firmware has kMaxVoicesPerInstrument 4 and
     EngineDef::maxVoices 4, 1 for PERC and T-Type). EngineDef::maxVoices is left as the firmware says so the host can offer Strict mode.
  3. open_load.h, open_synth.cpp: open::voicesHeld() for the stream frame header (keys down) and open::setHeldNotes(), which Strict mode
     (match my Tracker) uses to switch the held-note registry off, so A-Type then behaves as the firmware's.
No hunk touches the .patch codec (open_patchio.cpp): the editor saves and loads through the firmware's own code. The ids an older editor
build wrote (CHIP 0x10, D-Type 0x82) are mapped in host/poly_host.cpp before the codec sees them.
